export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"cabinetcraft","name":"CabinetCraft","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"cabinetcraft-start","scope":"addon:cabinetcraft","title":"CabinetCraft: getting started","summary":"What the CabinetCraft add-on does in PartCraft, BimCraft, CadCraft and ModelCraft, how to switch it on and how to lay out your first kitchen.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["cabinetcraft","cabinets","kitchen","millwork","casework","joinery","base cabinet","wall cabinet","tall cabinet","cut list","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-kitchen","text":"Your first kitchen","level":2},{"id":"units","text":"Units","level":2},{"id":"your-work-is-kept","text":"Your work is kept","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECabinetCraft is one add-on for four apps (PartCraft, BimCraft, CadCraft and ModelCraft) for designing a run of cabinets the way a cabinet shop works: a room (a rectangle, an L, a U or an outline of your own, with angled walls and curved bay walls) with its doors and windows, runs of base, wall and tall cabinets along its walls, islands, the boards and edge banding you use, and then everything the shop needs. From one job you get the cut list, a nest of the panels on sheets, a bar-coded label for every piece, 32 mm drilling, a hardware list, a cost estimate (a draft until the rates are your own), shop drawings (plan, elevations, sections and a 3D view), and then what your app can do with the cabinets: in PartCraft G-code for the sheets and the cabinets as bodies in the viewport; in BimCraft the cabinets as elements of the building model; in CadCraft the shop drawings as CAD entities; in ModelCraft the cabinets as bodies of the model. The job, the cut list, the nest, the drilling, the estimate and the drawings are the same in all four; the topic for your app says what it adds and what it cannot do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an add-on, not another app: it comes with PartCraft, is free and is off until you switch it on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinetCraft\u003C/span\u003E. Its tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinetCraft\u003C/span\u003E, is then in the right panel. It does not change your part&#039;s solid: a job is its own file. The one thing it puts in the part is the display bodies you ask for with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in viewport\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cabinets themselves are the ones CadCraft&#039;s Cabinets add-on builds: frameless boxes on the 32 mm system by default, and sides, bottom, rails or top, a back in grooves, shelves, doors and drawer boxes worked out from your construction numbers. CabinetCraft adds the designer&#039;s side of it: the room, the runs, a catalog of templates, face frames, blind corners, fillers, end panels, countertops, materials and the cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-kitchen\"\u003EYour first kitchen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob\u003C/span\u003E view and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExample kitchen\u003C/span\u003E to see a finished job, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew job\u003C/span\u003E to start empty. Then open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E and give the room&#039;s shape and size, its doors and windows, and any island.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, pick a run, then add cabinets: choose the type, check the width and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd cabinet\u003C/span\u003E. Cabinets go along the wall from its start corner in the order of the list; use the arrows to move one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGap\u003C/span\u003E leaves room for an appliance. A \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiller\u003C/span\u003E closes a small space. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill the rest\u003C/span\u003E adds a filler for what is left when it is 75 mm (3 in) or less.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E to see what is technically wrong: a run too long for its wall, a cabinet too narrow for its sides, a wall cabinet hung in front of a tall one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E shows the plan, an elevation of each run, a section of each kind of cabinet and a 3D view, and places the cabinets in the PartCraft viewport. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E nests the sheets and writes the files.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"units\"\u003EUnits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA job is in millimetres or inches, chosen in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob\u003C/span\u003E. Changing the unit converts every size (inches to a sixteenth) and puts the construction numbers back to that unit&#039;s own set.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-work-is-kept\"\u003EYour work is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe job is kept in this browser as you work. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the job file\u003C/span\u003E writes a \u003Ccode\u003E.cabinetcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file you can keep anywhere; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a job file\u003C/span\u003E reads it back. A file you open is checked and cleaned before it is used.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is CabinetCraft is one add-on for four apps (PartCraft, BimCraft, CadCraft and ModelCraft) for designing a run of cabinets the way a cabinet shop works: a room (a rectangle, an L, a U or an outline of your own, with angled walls and curved bay walls) with its doors and windows, runs of base, wall and tall cabinets along its walls, islands, the boards and edge banding you use, and then everything the shop needs. From one job you get the cut list, a nest of the panels on sheets, a bar-coded label for every piece, 32 mm drilling, a hardware list, a cost estimate (a draft until the rates are your own), shop drawings (plan, elevations, sections and a 3D view), and then what your app can do with the cabinets: in PartCraft G-code for the sheets and the cabinets as bodies in the viewport; in BimCraft the cabinets as elements of the building model; in CadCraft the shop drawings as CAD entities; in ModelCraft the cabinets as bodies of the model. The job, the cut list, the nest, the drilling, the estimate and the drawings are the same in all four; the topic for your app says what it adds and what it cannot do. It is an add-on, not another app: it comes with PartCraft, is free and is off until you switch it on in Settings › General › Add-ons › CabinetCraft. Its tab, CabinetCraft, is then in the right panel. It does not change your part's solid: a job is its own file. The one thing it puts in the part is the display bodies you ask for with Place in viewport. The cabinets themselves are the ones CadCraft's Cabinets add-on builds: frameless boxes on the 32 mm system by default, and sides, bottom, rails or top, a back in grooves, shelves, doors and drawer boxes worked out from your construction numbers. CabinetCraft adds the designer's side of it: the room, the runs, a catalog of templates, face frames, blind corners, fillers, end panels, countertops, materials and the cost. Your first kitchen Open the Job view and press Example kitchen to see a finished job, or New job to start empty. Then open Room and give the room's shape and size, its doors and windows, and any island. In Layout, pick a run, then add cabinets: choose the type, check the width and press Add cabinet. Cabinets go along the wall from its start corner in the order of the list; use the arrows to move one. A Gap leaves room for an appliance. A Filler closes a small space. Fill the rest adds a filler for what is left when it is 75 mm (3 in) or less. Open Checks to see what is technically wrong: a run too long for its wall, a cabinet too narrow for its sides, a wall cabinet hung in front of a tall one. Drawings shows the plan, an elevation of each run, a section of each kind of cabinet and a 3D view, and places the cabinets in the PartCraft viewport. Export nests the sheets and writes the files. Units A job is in millimetres or inches, chosen in Job. Changing the unit converts every size (inches to a sixteenth) and puts the construction numbers back to that unit's own set. Your work is kept The job is kept in this browser as you work. Save the job file writes a .cabinetcraft.json file you can keep anywhere; Open a job file reads it back. A file you open is checked and cleaned before it is used."},{"slug":"cabinetcraft-layout","scope":"addon:cabinetcraft","title":"CabinetCraft: rooms, runs and cabinets","summary":"How a room (rectangle, L, U, free outline with angled and curved walls), its doors and windows, its runs and islands are laid out, the catalog, corner cabinets, face frames, fillers, end panels and countertops.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["run","room","angled wall","curved wall","bay","bow","arc","wedge","mitre","L-shaped","U-shaped","outline","island","peninsula","door","window","lazy susan","wall","corner","filler","end panel","countertop","face frame","frameless","blind corner","template","catalog"],"headings":[{"id":"the-room","text":"The room","level":2},{"id":"angled-and-curved-walls","text":"Angled and curved walls","level":3},{"id":"runs","text":"Runs","level":2},{"id":"runs-on-angled-and-curved-walls","text":"Runs on angled and curved walls","level":2},{"id":"corner-cabinets-that-turn-the-corner","text":"Corner cabinets that turn the corner","level":2},{"id":"islands-and-peninsulas","text":"Islands and peninsulas","level":2},{"id":"doors-and-windows","text":"Doors and windows","level":2},{"id":"the-catalog","text":"The catalog","level":2},{"id":"frameless-or-face-frame","text":"Frameless or face frame","level":2},{"id":"blind-corners","text":"Blind corners","level":2},{"id":"fillers-end-panels-countertops","text":"Fillers, end panels, countertops","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-know","text":"What it does not know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-room\"\u003EThe room\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E view sets the room&#039;s shape: a \u003Cstrong\u003Erectangle\u003C/strong\u003E, an \u003Cstrong\u003EL\u003C/strong\u003E (the rectangle with a notch cut from a corner), a \u003Cstrong\u003EU\u003C/strong\u003E (a notch cut from the middle of a side) or a \u003Cstrong\u003Efree outline\u003C/strong\u003E with right angles. A free outline is typed, one corner per line as x, y, or picked: click the corners in order round the room on the small plan, each snaps to 50 mm (2 in) and to a straight line from the one before, then press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose outline\u003C/span\u003E. Edges that cross are refused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"angled-and-curved-walls\"\u003EAngled and curved walls\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngled and curved walls\u003C/span\u003E type the walls one a line from the first corner: \u003Ccode\u003Elength angle\u003C/code\u003E for a straight wall (the angle is its direction in degrees, 0 east, 90 north), or \u003Ccode\u003Earc radius sweep heading\u003C/code\u003E for an arc (sweep + bulges away from the room, a bay, and - into it; the heading is the direction from where it starts to where it ends). The outline closes itself. Or tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAny angle\u003C/span\u003E on the plan and click the corners, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc walls\u003C/span\u003E to click a point on an arc and then where it ends. Or take a wall of a free outline and make it an arc of a sweep you give, and straight again. Walls then say their direction and how far the room turns at each corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe walls go round counter-clockwise. A rectangle&#039;s are the south, east, north and west walls; an L, U or free outline has Wall 1, Wall 2 and so on. A corner where the walls turn away from the room (the inside corner of an L) has no wall behind the end of the run that reaches it, so that end gets an end panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"runs\"\u003ERuns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Erun\u003C/strong\u003E lies along one wall and starts at that wall&#039;s start corner. Each run has two rows: the \u003Cstrong\u003Efloor row\u003C/strong\u003E (base, drawer and tall cabinets) and the \u003Cstrong\u003Eupper row\u003C/strong\u003E (wall cabinets). Both are measured along the wall from the start of the run.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere two walls meet in a room corner and both runs reach it, the run listed later stops short by the depth of the one listed first, so their backs butt and nothing overlaps. List the run that keeps the corner first. If the second run was designed to the full wall it goes over, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E says by how much.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"runs-on-angled-and-curved-walls\"\u003ERuns on angled and curved walls\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run on a slanted wall is turned to follow it: its cabinets, end panels and fillers are rotated, and the checks, plan, elevation, cut list, 3D drawing and viewport bodies all follow. Where two runs meet at a corner that is not a right angle, the later run stops short by \u003Cstrong\u003Edepth x sin(the turn)\u003C/strong\u003E (the same as the depth at a right angle). What is left behind is a right triangle; a \u003Cstrong\u003Eclosing panel\u003C/strong\u003E (W01, W02 ...) shuts the end of the other run, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E gives the legs of the triangle behind it. An inside angle under 90 degrees has no stock form: it is an error that says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurved wall\u003C/strong\u003E the cabinets stay straight and are stepped along the curve, each turned to the tangent at its own middle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebay\u003C/strong\u003E (the centre of the curve in the room) the back corners of each cabinet touch the wall, the middle of its back is a sagitta from the curve (the largest gap, stated in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E), neighbours just clear each other at their fronts and a wedge-shaped void is left at the wall behind them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebow\u003C/strong\u003E (the wall bulges into the room) the middle of each back touches the wall, neighbours just clear at their backs, and a wedge gap opens at the fronts: close it with wedge fillers or choose narrower cabinets. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E warns.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFewer cabinets fit than their widths add up to, because of the wedges. A run that goes past the end of the curve is an error with the amount.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurved-front cabinet\u003C/strong\u003E (Base, curved front) is a sector of the ring, its width an arc length. It is \u003Cstrong\u003Emade to order\u003C/strong\u003E: flagged in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E, a segmented body in the drawings and viewport, a placeholder line in the estimate by the metre of front arc, no cut list or drilling. Its front radius is shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA countertop on a curve is one piece following the cabinets&#039; corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDoors and windows work on angled walls; on a curve they sit at positions along the chord (the straight line between its corners).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-cabinets-that-turn-the-corner\"\u003ECorner cabinets that turn the corner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA corner cabinet is the first or last item of a run that reaches a room corner. It fills the corner square and has an arm along each wall, so the next wall&#039;s run starts after the arm instead of after a depth. Three kinds, in base and (L-shaped and diagonal) wall sizes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EL-shaped:\u003C/strong\u003E an arm on each wall, a door on each arm. Its bottom, top and shelves are cut as two rectangles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDiagonal front:\u003C/strong\u003E the two arms and one diagonal door. Bottom, top and shelves have a triangle blank, cut on the diagonal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELazy susan:\u003C/strong\u003E the diagonal cabinet with two round shelves on a pole; the shelf is cut from a square blank.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe corner cabinet has a countertop of its own with the shape of its footprint. A gap before it that is 75 mm (3 in) or less becomes a filler beside it; any more is left as a gap. Corner cabinets are drilled: see the export topic.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt a corner that is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E a right angle only the \u003Cstrong\u003Ediagonal front\u003C/strong\u003E cabinet is made, at the true angle: two arm boards joined in a mitre, a triangle closing the front and one door as long as the front. The L-shaped and lazy susan cabinets only fit a right angle and are laid out as plain cabinets with an error. A corner cabinet needs two straight walls. The common-practice sizes are in numbers you can change, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E says when an arm is too short for a door.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"islands-and-peninsulas\"\u003EIslands and peninsulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn island is a free-standing run: set where its back line starts (x, y), which way it runs and how long it is in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E, then lay out its cabinets in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E like a run. The cabinets face the left of the back line; a second row on the \u003Cstrong\u003Eback\u003C/strong\u003E faces the other way. A countertop overhang over the back is the seating. An end against a wall makes it a peninsula: no end panel and no overhang at that end. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E warns when the walk-around space is under 900 mm (36 in), a common-practice figure and not a code.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA door or window is placed on a wall, measured along it from the wall&#039;s start corner, with its width, height and (for a window) the sill height. Cabinets respect them:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA cabinet that stands in front of a door, or a pantry as tall as a window, is an error. Within 50 mm (2 in) of a door frame is a warning, and so is a cabinet in the swing of a door that opens into the room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA base cabinet under a window is fine as long as the sill is above it. A wall cabinet in front of the glass is an error; one hung below a window is flagged.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave it free in run\u003C/span\u003E puts a gap in the run&#039;s floor row where the opening is, so the cabinets after it move on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-catalog\"\u003EThe catalog\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwenty templates give you a starting point: base cabinets with one door, a pair, for a sink, with two, three or four drawers and a blind corner; wall cabinets with one door, a pair, open, short for over a fridge or hood and a blind corner; tall pantry and broom cabinets; corner cabinets that turn the corner, and a made-to-order curved-front base cabinet for a curved wall. Every number is yours to change after you pick one: the gear on a cabinet opens its height, depth, shelves, doors, drawers, hinge side, blind part and a note.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"frameless-or-face-frame\"\u003EFrameless or face frame\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe job has a construction, and any one cabinet can differ.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrameless (32 mm system):\u003C/strong\u003E full-overlay doors and drawer fronts, the boxes carrying the hinges on the sides.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFace frame:\u003C/strong\u003E a frame of stiles and rails (hardwood, cut by length, listed apart from the sheets) on the front of a box that is shallower by the frame&#039;s thickness. Doors and drawer fronts overlay the frame&#039;s openings by the amount you set, with a gap between them. Hinge plates go on the frame, so the sides carry no plate holes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe frame numbers (stile, rail, overlay, gap) are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstruction numbers\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"blind-corners\"\u003EBlind corners\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA blind corner cabinet has a closed part of a width you set. The door is on the open part, hinged on the side away from the blind part, and a blind panel closes the front of the rest. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E warns when the blind part is too short for the next run&#039;s door to clear it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fillers-end-panels-countertops\"\u003EFillers, end panels, countertops\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Efiller\u003C/strong\u003E is a flat strip closing a small space. With the run&#039;s automatic filler on, a leftover up to 75 mm (3 in) becomes one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Eend panel\u003C/strong\u003E finishes an open end of a row. It covers the cabinet&#039;s depth and the front, and is made when the end is not against a wall or the next run (you can force it on or off for each end).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ecountertop\u003C/strong\u003E piece is worked out for each stretch of base cabinets, fillers and appliance gaps that carry the counter: its length with the overhang at an open end, its width with the front overhang. It is priced in the cost, not cut from sheets. A gap marked as an appliance space keeps the counter going over it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-know\"\u003EWhat it does not know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sizes are common shop practice in numbers you can change. They are not tied to any maker&#039;s hinges, slides or legs: check them before cutting. Drawings are made from the same cabinets as the cut list, so they cannot disagree with it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The room The Room view sets the room's shape: a rectangle, an L (the rectangle with a notch cut from a corner), a U (a notch cut from the middle of a side) or a free outline with right angles. A free outline is typed, one corner per line as x, y, or picked: click the corners in order round the room on the small plan, each snaps to 50 mm (2 in) and to a straight line from the one before, then press Close outline. Edges that cross are refused. Angled and curved walls Under Angled and curved walls type the walls one a line from the first corner: length angle for a straight wall (the angle is its direction in degrees, 0 east, 90 north), or arc radius sweep heading for an arc (sweep + bulges away from the room, a bay, and - into it; the heading is the direction from where it starts to where it ends). The outline closes itself. Or tick Any angle on the plan and click the corners, and Arc walls to click a point on an arc and then where it ends. Or take a wall of a free outline and make it an arc of a sweep you give, and straight again. Walls then say their direction and how far the room turns at each corner. The walls go round counter-clockwise. A rectangle's are the south, east, north and west walls; an L, U or free outline has Wall 1, Wall 2 and so on. A corner where the walls turn away from the room (the inside corner of an L) has no wall behind the end of the run that reaches it, so that end gets an end panel. Runs A run lies along one wall and starts at that wall's start corner. Each run has two rows: the floor row (base, drawer and tall cabinets) and the upper row (wall cabinets). Both are measured along the wall from the start of the run. Where two walls meet in a room corner and both runs reach it, the run listed later stops short by the depth of the one listed first, so their backs butt and nothing overlaps. List the run that keeps the corner first. If the second run was designed to the full wall it goes over, and Checks says by how much. Runs on angled and curved walls A run on a slanted wall is turned to follow it: its cabinets, end panels and fillers are rotated, and the checks, plan, elevation, cut list, 3D drawing and viewport bodies all follow. Where two runs meet at a corner that is not a right angle, the later run stops short by depth x sin(the turn) (the same as the depth at a right angle). What is left behind is a right triangle; a closing panel (W01, W02 ...) shuts the end of the other run, and Checks gives the legs of the triangle behind it. An inside angle under 90 degrees has no stock form: it is an error that says so. On a curved wall the cabinets stay straight and are stepped along the curve, each turned to the tangent at its own middle. On a bay (the centre of the curve in the room) the back corners of each cabinet touch the wall, the middle of its back is a sagitta from the curve (the largest gap, stated in Checks), neighbours just clear each other at their fronts and a wedge-shaped void is left at the wall behind them. On a bow (the wall bulges into the room) the middle of each back touches the wall, neighbours just clear at their backs, and a wedge gap opens at the fronts: close it with wedge fillers or choose narrower cabinets. Checks warns. Fewer cabinets fit than their widths add up to, because of the wedges. A run that goes past the end of the curve is an error with the amount. A curved-front cabinet (Base, curved front) is a sector of the ring, its width an arc length. It is made to order: flagged in Checks, a segmented body in the drawings and viewport, a placeholder line in the estimate by the metre of front arc, no cut list or drilling. Its front radius is shown. A countertop on a curve is one piece following the cabinets' corners. Doors and windows work on angled walls; on a curve they sit at positions along the chord (the straight line between its corners). Corner cabinets that turn the corner A corner cabinet is the first or last item of a run that reaches a room corner. It fills the corner square and has an arm along each wall, so the next wall's run starts after the arm instead of after a depth. Three kinds, in base and (L-shaped and diagonal) wall sizes: L-shaped: an arm on each wall, a door on each arm. Its bottom, top and shelves are cut as two rectangles. Diagonal front: the two arms and one diagonal door. Bottom, top and shelves have a triangle blank, cut on the diagonal. Lazy susan: the diagonal cabinet with two round shelves on a pole; the shelf is cut from a square blank. The corner cabinet has a countertop of its own with the shape of its footprint. A gap before it that is 75 mm (3 in) or less becomes a filler beside it; any more is left as a gap. Corner cabinets are drilled: see the export topic. At a corner that is not a right angle only the diagonal front cabinet is made, at the true angle: two arm boards joined in a mitre, a triangle closing the front and one door as long as the front. The L-shaped and lazy susan cabinets only fit a right angle and are laid out as plain cabinets with an error. A corner cabinet needs two straight walls. The common-practice sizes are in numbers you can change, and Checks says when an arm is too short for a door. Islands and peninsulas An island is a free-standing run: set where its back line starts (x, y), which way it runs and how long it is in Room, then lay out its cabinets in Layout like a run. The cabinets face the left of the back line; a second row on the back faces the other way. A countertop overhang over the back is the seating. An end against a wall makes it a peninsula: no end panel and no overhang at that end. Checks warns when the walk-around space is under 900 mm (36 in), a common-practice figure and not a code. Doors and windows A door or window is placed on a wall, measured along it from the wall's start corner, with its width, height and (for a window) the sill height. Cabinets respect them: A cabinet that stands in front of a door, or a pantry as tall as a window, is an error. Within 50 mm (2 in) of a door frame is a warning, and so is a cabinet in the swing of a door that opens into the room. A base cabinet under a window is fine as long as the sill is above it. A wall cabinet in front of the glass is an error; one hung below a window is flagged. Leave it free in run puts a gap in the run's floor row where the opening is, so the cabinets after it move on. The catalog Twenty templates give you a starting point: base cabinets with one door, a pair, for a sink, with two, three or four drawers and a blind corner; wall cabinets with one door, a pair, open, short for over a fridge or hood and a blind corner; tall pantry and broom cabinets; corner cabinets that turn the corner, and a made-to-order curved-front base cabinet for a curved wall. Every number is yours to change after you pick one: the gear on a cabinet opens its height, depth, shelves, doors, drawers, hinge side, blind part and a note. Frameless or face frame The job has a construction, and any one cabinet can differ. Frameless (32 mm system): full-overlay doors and drawer fronts, the boxes carrying the hinges on the sides. Face frame: a frame of stiles and rails (hardwood, cut by length, listed apart from the sheets) on the front of a box that is shallower by the frame's thickness. Doors and drawer fronts overlay the frame's openings by the amount you set, with a gap between them. Hinge plates go on the frame, so the sides carry no plate holes. The frame numbers (stile, rail, overlay, gap) are in Materials › Construction numbers. Blind corners A blind corner cabinet has a closed part of a width you set. The door is on the open part, hinged on the side away from the blind part, and a blind panel closes the front of the rest. Checks warns when the blind part is too short for the next run's door to clear it. Fillers, end panels, countertops A filler is a flat strip closing a small space. With the run's automatic filler on, a leftover up to 75 mm (3 in) becomes one. An end panel finishes an open end of a row. It covers the cabinet's depth and the front, and is made when the end is not against a wall or the next run (you can force it on or off for each end). A countertop piece is worked out for each stretch of base cabinets, fillers and appliance gaps that carry the counter: its length with the overhang at an open end, its width with the front overhang. It is priced in the cost, not cut from sheets. A gap marked as an appliance space keeps the counter going over it. What it does not know The sizes are common shop practice in numbers you can change. They are not tied to any maker's hinges, slides or legs: check them before cutting. Drawings are made from the same cabinets as the cut list, so they cannot disagree with it."},{"slug":"cabinetcraft-materials-cost","scope":"addon:cabinetcraft","title":"CabinetCraft: materials and cost","summary":"Picking boards and edge banding, your own materials, the construction numbers, and the cost estimate with its placeholder rates.","section":"tasks","order":3,"keywords":["materials","edge banding","sheet size","cost","estimate","rates","placeholder","hardware","labour"],"headings":[{"id":"materials","text":"Materials","level":2},{"id":"construction-numbers","text":"Construction numbers","level":2},{"id":"cost-estimate","text":"Cost estimate","level":2},{"id":"your-shop-s-own-rates","text":"Your shop's own rates","level":2},{"id":"the-first-run-and-the-final-quote","text":"The first run, and the final quote","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"materials\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/span\u003E lists generic boards by kind and thickness, with no maker named: melamine and plywood carcass boards, fibreboard and plywood backs, fronts, drawer sides and bottoms, hardwood for a face frame, and edge bands. A pick sets the thickness in the construction rules (a 15 mm carcass makes 15 mm sides) and the name in the cut list. Each material has its own sheet, and the nest uses it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd your own under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour own materials\u003C/span\u003E: a name as your shop writes it, what it is used for, its thickness and, if you like, its sheet size. A material of your own keeps its name exactly as typed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"construction-numbers\"\u003EConstruction numbers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlinth height, how far the plinth sits back, the gap between fronts, the height wall cabinets hang at, how far shelves stop short of the front, the widest and tallest single door and the drawer slide gap are all numbers you can change. The default for each is shown when you hold the pointer over it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cost-estimate\"\u003ECost estimate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECost\u003C/span\u003E adds up sheets, edge banding, face-frame hardwood, hardware, countertop, shop labour by kind of cabinet and machine time, then a markup and tax.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery rate starts as a placeholder.\u003C/strong\u003E The numbers shipped with the add-on are round figures in one currency so the arithmetic works; they are not a price list and not anyone&#039;s prices. A placeholder is marked with a star in the table, the page says so in capitals while any are left, and the CSV has a column and a closing note that say so. When you put in your own rate for a line it stops being a placeholder. Until your shop has set its own, treat the total as an example of how the estimate works, not as a quote.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-shop-s-own-rates\"\u003EYour shop&#039;s own rates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo replace the placeholders, type your rate for a line under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERates\u003C/span\u003E, or import a table of your own: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload the rate sheet\u003C/span\u003E gives a CSV of every rate (its path, its value now and whether it is still a placeholder); fill in your prices, using a decimal point, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport my rates\u003C/span\u003E. A CSV of \u003Ccode\u003Epath,value\u003C/code\u003E lines or a JSON file in the job&#039;s own shape also works. Only the rates the file names are replaced, a line that is not a rate or not a usable number is listed and ignored, and the rest stay placeholders. Your rates are saved with the job and with the job file. CabinetCraft supplies no rate of its own that is anyone&#039;s price.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-first-run-and-the-final-quote\"\u003EThe first run, and the final quote\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first time you open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECost\u003C/span\u003E on a job whose rates you have not touched, it asks \u003Cstrong\u003EType your shop&#039;s rates\u003C/strong\u003E: type them under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERates\u003C/span\u003E, or download the rate sheet, fill it in and import it. The estimate carries a label. It says \u003Cstrong\u003EDRAFT\u003C/strong\u003E while any rate in use is a placeholder, and the lint under it lists the categories that still are (sheet materials, banding, hardware, labour, machine time, countertop, made-to-order cabinets, markup and tax). It says \u003Cstrong\u003EFINAL QUOTE\u003C/strong\u003E only when every rate the job uses is yours, and the estimate CSV ends with the same label. A rate counts as yours when you typed or imported it, even if it happens to equal the placeholder (a tax of 0, say). The label is a check on the rates, not on the arithmetic or the quantities: have the numbers looked at before a quote goes out. The real rates are your shop&#039;s to supply; CabinetCraft supplies none.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets are counted from the nest when you have nested the job, else estimated from the area of the parts with 15 % for the saw kerf and waste.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Materials Materials lists generic boards by kind and thickness, with no maker named: melamine and plywood carcass boards, fibreboard and plywood backs, fronts, drawer sides and bottoms, hardwood for a face frame, and edge bands. A pick sets the thickness in the construction rules (a 15 mm carcass makes 15 mm sides) and the name in the cut list. Each material has its own sheet, and the nest uses it. Add your own under Your own materials: a name as your shop writes it, what it is used for, its thickness and, if you like, its sheet size. A material of your own keeps its name exactly as typed. Construction numbers Plinth height, how far the plinth sits back, the gap between fronts, the height wall cabinets hang at, how far shelves stop short of the front, the widest and tallest single door and the drawer slide gap are all numbers you can change. The default for each is shown when you hold the pointer over it. Cost estimate Cost adds up sheets, edge banding, face-frame hardwood, hardware, countertop, shop labour by kind of cabinet and machine time, then a markup and tax. Every rate starts as a placeholder. The numbers shipped with the add-on are round figures in one currency so the arithmetic works; they are not a price list and not anyone's prices. A placeholder is marked with a star in the table, the page says so in capitals while any are left, and the CSV has a column and a closing note that say so. When you put in your own rate for a line it stops being a placeholder. Until your shop has set its own, treat the total as an example of how the estimate works, not as a quote. Your shop's own rates To replace the placeholders, type your rate for a line under Rates, or import a table of your own: Download the rate sheet gives a CSV of every rate (its path, its value now and whether it is still a placeholder); fill in your prices, using a decimal point, and Import my rates. A CSV of path,value lines or a JSON file in the job's own shape also works. Only the rates the file names are replaced, a line that is not a rate or not a usable number is listed and ignored, and the rest stay placeholders. Your rates are saved with the job and with the job file. CabinetCraft supplies no rate of its own that is anyone's price. The first run, and the final quote The first time you open Cost on a job whose rates you have not touched, it asks Type your shop's rates: type them under Rates, or download the rate sheet, fill it in and import it. The estimate carries a label. It says DRAFT while any rate in use is a placeholder, and the lint under it lists the categories that still are (sheet materials, banding, hardware, labour, machine time, countertop, made-to-order cabinets, markup and tax). It says FINAL QUOTE only when every rate the job uses is yours, and the estimate CSV ends with the same label. A rate counts as yours when you typed or imported it, even if it happens to equal the placeholder (a tax of 0, say). The label is a check on the rates, not on the arithmetic or the quantities: have the numbers looked at before a quote goes out. The real rates are your shop's to supply; CabinetCraft supplies none. Sheets are counted from the nest when you have nested the job, else estimated from the area of the parts with 15 % for the saw kerf and waste."},{"slug":"cabinetcraft-export","scope":"addon:cabinetcraft","title":"CabinetCraft: cut list, nest, labels and G-code","summary":"The files a job gives the shop, drilling for corner cabinets, the dry-run checker, the first-run checklist and the maker's review sheet.","section":"tasks","order":4,"keywords":["dry run","checklist","maker review","corner drilling","dowel","cut list","csv","nest","dxf","labels","bar code","drilling","hardware list","g-code","nested sheets","post"],"headings":[{"id":"the-files","text":"The files","level":2},{"id":"nest-on-sheets","text":"Nest on sheets","level":2},{"id":"g-code","text":"G-code","level":2},{"id":"the-generic-hole-list-xml","text":"The generic hole list (XML)","level":2},{"id":"the-cabinets-in-the-viewport","text":"The cabinets in the viewport","level":2},{"id":"drilling-the-corner-cabinets","text":"Drilling the corner cabinets","level":2},{"id":"dry-run-the-first-run-checklist-and-the-maker-s-review-sheet","text":"Dry run, the first-run checklist and the maker's review sheet","level":2},{"id":"not-built","text":"Not built","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-files\"\u003EThe files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E gives you:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut list (CSV):\u003C/strong\u003E every part with its material, length, width, quantity, grain and the edge bands on each side, with the size to cut once the banding is taken off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrilling list (CSV):\u003C/strong\u003E the shelf-pin, hinge-plate, cup-hinge and drawer-slide holes on the 32 mm system, by part and face, and the corner cabinets&#039; holes (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrilling as XML (generic):\u003C/strong\u003E the same holes, a panel at a time, in a format of our own that is described below. It is not any boring machine&#039;s file format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHardware list (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECost estimate (CSV).\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELabels:\u003C/strong\u003E a printable page of labels, one for each piece, with its name, cabinet, size, material, a drawing of its banded edges and a Code 128 bar code. After a nest the labels are in cutting order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENested sheets (DXF):\u003C/strong\u003E every sheet of every material, with layers for the sheet, the parts and the labels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nest-on-sheets\"\u003ENest on sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest on sheets\u003C/span\u003E lays the parts of each material on that material&#039;s sheet, keeping the grain. It runs on a Web Worker, so the page stays usable while a bar shows which material it is on; when a page cannot start a worker it runs on the page&#039;s own thread, one material at a time, and the page is slow to answer for a few seconds. Parts that do not fit their sheet are listed and shown in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E. Any edit that changes the parts drops the nest, because it would no longer match.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"g-code\"\u003EG-code\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake the G-code\u003C/span\u003E uses PartCraft&#039;s nested-sheets path, the same one as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EManufacture\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENested sheets to G-code\u003C/span\u003E: each material&#039;s sheets go through the shared contour operation (offset by the tool radius, climbing, lead arcs, tabs) and the post you pick, one program per sheet. The cut depth is the board&#039;s thickness.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing here has been run on a machine. Check every program in a simulator and on a scrap board before you cut, and check that the tool, the speeds and the post fit your machine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-generic-hole-list-xml\"\u003EThe generic hole list (XML)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file&#039;s root is \u003Ccode\u003Ecabinetcraft-holes\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Eformat\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eversion\u003C/code\u003E (1), \u003Ccode\u003Egeneric=&quot;true&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eunit\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003Emm\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Ein\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode\u003Ejob\u003C/code\u003E. Each \u003Ccode\u003Epanel\u003C/code\u003E has \u003Ccode\u003Eid\u003C/code\u003E (the cut list&#039;s part id), \u003Ccode\u003Ecabinet\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eqty\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ethickness\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ematerial\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eface\u003C/code\u003E (which face of the panel the holes are on). Each \u003Ccode\u003Ehole\u003C/code\u003E inside has \u003Ccode\u003Ekind\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003Eshelf-pin\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eplate\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eslide\u003C/code\u003E, and for corner cabinets \u003Ccode\u003Efasten\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edowel\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Epivot\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Eshaft\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode\u003Ex\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ey\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ediameter\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edepth\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eside\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003Eboth\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eleft\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Eright\u003C/code\u003E: which of a pair of panels takes it). \u003Cstrong\u003Ex\u003C/strong\u003E runs along the panel&#039;s length from the end the cut list starts at, \u003Cstrong\u003Ey\u003C/strong\u003E across its width from the first long edge, both in the file&#039;s unit. A shop&#039;s own software, or a post-processor someone writes, can read it; nothing here has been run on a machine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-cabinets-in-the-viewport\"\u003EThe cabinets in the viewport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in viewport\u003C/span\u003E puts the job in PartCraft&#039;s 3D view as bodies: one for every panel, door, drawer front, filler, end panel and countertop piece, coloured by your material picks, with the floor and the walls (translucent, with the doors and windows cut in) if you leave that box ticked. It is one undo step; placing again replaces the group and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove from viewport\u003C/span\u003E takes it out. The command \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAB3D\u003C/span\u003E does the same from PartCraft&#039;s command list. They are for looking at: display bodies are kept in the part file but are not part of the part&#039;s solid, so they add nothing to its mass, an STL or a STEP file. The SVG 3D drawing is still there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drilling-the-corner-cabinets\"\u003EDrilling the corner cabinets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EL-shaped, diagonal-front (at any angle), lazy susan and blind cabinets are drilled with the same numbers as the straight ones (the 32 mm system, changeable under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/span\u003E):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnd sides:\u003C/strong\u003E shelf pins on the lines \u003Ccode\u003EsysLine\u003C/code\u003E (37 mm) from the front and back edges, every \u003Ccode\u003Epitch\u003C/code\u003E (32 mm), the first \u003Ccode\u003EsysStart\u003C/code\u003E (64 mm) from the end; hinge plates, two system holes for each hinge, on the side that carries the hinge (both for the L-shaped cabinet&#039;s two doors); four fixing holes where the bottom and top meet the side, \u003Ccode\u003EsysStart\u003C/code\u003E from each edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBoards that meet:\u003C/strong\u003E a hole in the EDGE (face \u003Ccode\u003Eedge\u003C/code\u003E, with the edge number) at \u003Ccode\u003EsysStart\u003C/code\u003E from each end of every joint line two boards share (one at the middle if the joint is shorter than 2 x \u003Ccode\u003EsysStart\u003C/code\u003E + \u003Ccode\u003Epitch\u003C/code\u003E), and two where an arm board meets a side. At an angled corner the joints are the \u003Cstrong\u003Emitre\u003C/strong\u003E between the arm boards and the sides of the triangle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDoors:\u003C/strong\u003E cup hinges on the back, \u003Ccode\u003EcupEdge\u003C/code\u003E + half the cup from the hinge edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELazy susan:\u003C/strong\u003E a pivot hole in the bottom, a shaft hole in the top and a centre hole in each round shelf.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe size of a \u003Cstrong\u003Efixing hole\u003C/strong\u003E (dowel or cam) and of a lazy susan&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Eshaft and pivot\u003C/strong\u003E depends on the hardware you chose. Type them under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrilling sizes you type\u003C/span\u003E; until you do the holes are in the list with a \u003Cstrong\u003Eblank\u003C/strong\u003E diameter and depth, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E says so. Nothing is invented. A dowel-or-cam pair per two holes is in the hardware list. In the XML a hole may carry \u003Ccode\u003Eface\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eedge\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dry-run-the-first-run-checklist-and-the-maker-s-review-sheet\"\u003EDry run, the first-run checklist and the maker&#039;s review sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENone of these is a machine run, and none says one happened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the programs and holes\u003C/span\u003E reads each G-code program like a very small simulator and reports its bounding box, tools and tool changes, the feed rates, the length of cutting and fast travel and the time (cutting only, or with the fast-move rate you type), and finds: cutting with no feed, fast sideways moves below the top of the sheet, cuts outside the sheet, wrong units, no tool or spindle, and cuts that do not go through. It also checks that \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery hole of the hole list lies inside its panel\u003C/strong\u003E, keeps at least the smallest edge distance the drilling rules themselves use (the cup edge) from each edge, is not deeper than the panel is thick, and lists the sizes still blank.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst machine run checklist\u003C/span\u003E is one printable page: the programs, what the dry run saw, what to check before the sheet goes on and in the first cuts, empty boxes and a sign-off. It says that no run has been made.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaker&#039;s review sheet\u003C/span\u003E lists every size and rule the corner cabinets, angled and curved walls and drilling used, where each comes from (common practice, a rule of the 32 mm system, geometry, typed by you) and an empty &quot;maker confirms&quot; box and &quot;corrects to&quot; space for a cabinet maker.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built\"\u003ENot built\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe labels have a Code 128 bar code and no QR code. The hole list is a generic format, not a boring machine&#039;s own file. The display bodies have no joinery detail: a panel is a box. A cabinet with a curved front is made to order and is not in the cut list, the programs or the hole list.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The files Export gives you: Cut list (CSV): every part with its material, length, width, quantity, grain and the edge bands on each side, with the size to cut once the banding is taken off. Drilling list (CSV): the shelf-pin, hinge-plate, cup-hinge and drawer-slide holes on the 32 mm system, by part and face, and the corner cabinets' holes (below). Drilling as XML (generic): the same holes, a panel at a time, in a format of our own that is described below. It is not any boring machine's file format. Hardware list (CSV) and Cost estimate (CSV). Labels: a printable page of labels, one for each piece, with its name, cabinet, size, material, a drawing of its banded edges and a Code 128 bar code. After a nest the labels are in cutting order. Nested sheets (DXF): every sheet of every material, with layers for the sheet, the parts and the labels. Nest on sheets Nest on sheets lays the parts of each material on that material's sheet, keeping the grain. It runs on a Web Worker, so the page stays usable while a bar shows which material it is on; when a page cannot start a worker it runs on the page's own thread, one material at a time, and the page is slow to answer for a few seconds. Parts that do not fit their sheet are listed and shown in Checks. Any edit that changes the parts drops the nest, because it would no longer match. G-code Make the G-code uses PartCraft's nested-sheets path, the same one as Manufacture › Nested sheets to G-code: each material's sheets go through the shared contour operation (offset by the tool radius, climbing, lead arcs, tabs) and the post you pick, one program per sheet. The cut depth is the board's thickness. Nothing here has been run on a machine. Check every program in a simulator and on a scrap board before you cut, and check that the tool, the speeds and the post fit your machine. The generic hole list (XML) The file's root is cabinetcraft-holes with format, version (1), generic=\"true\", unit (mm or in) and job. Each panel has id (the cut list's part id), cabinet, name, qty, length, width, thickness, material and face (which face of the panel the holes are on). Each hole inside has kind (shelf-pin, plate, cup, slide, and for corner cabinets fasten, dowel, pivot or shaft), x, y, diameter, depth and side (both, left or right: which of a pair of panels takes it). x runs along the panel's length from the end the cut list starts at, y across its width from the first long edge, both in the file's unit. A shop's own software, or a post-processor someone writes, can read it; nothing here has been run on a machine. The cabinets in the viewport On Drawings, Place in viewport puts the job in PartCraft's 3D view as bodies: one for every panel, door, drawer front, filler, end panel and countertop piece, coloured by your material picks, with the floor and the walls (translucent, with the doors and windows cut in) if you leave that box ticked. It is one undo step; placing again replaces the group and Remove from viewport takes it out. The command CAB3D does the same from PartCraft's command list. They are for looking at: display bodies are kept in the part file but are not part of the part's solid, so they add nothing to its mass, an STL or a STEP file. The SVG 3D drawing is still there. Drilling the corner cabinets L-shaped, diagonal-front (at any angle), lazy susan and blind cabinets are drilled with the same numbers as the straight ones (the 32 mm system, changeable under Materials): End sides: shelf pins on the lines sysLine (37 mm) from the front and back edges, every pitch (32 mm), the first sysStart (64 mm) from the end; hinge plates, two system holes for each hinge, on the side that carries the hinge (both for the L-shaped cabinet's two doors); four fixing holes where the bottom and top meet the side, sysStart from each edge. Boards that meet: a hole in the EDGE (face edge, with the edge number) at sysStart from each end of every joint line two boards share (one at the middle if the joint is shorter than 2 x sysStart + pitch), and two where an arm board meets a side. At an angled corner the joints are the mitre between the arm boards and the sides of the triangle. Doors: cup hinges on the back, cupEdge + half the cup from the hinge edge. Lazy susan: a pivot hole in the bottom, a shaft hole in the top and a centre hole in each round shelf. The size of a fixing hole (dowel or cam) and of a lazy susan's shaft and pivot depends on the hardware you chose. Type them under Drilling sizes you type; until you do the holes are in the list with a blank diameter and depth, and Checks says so. Nothing is invented. A dowel-or-cam pair per two holes is in the hardware list. In the XML a hole may carry face and edge. Dry run, the first-run checklist and the maker's review sheet None of these is a machine run, and none says one happened. Check the programs and holes reads each G-code program like a very small simulator and reports its bounding box, tools and tool changes, the feed rates, the length of cutting and fast travel and the time (cutting only, or with the fast-move rate you type), and finds: cutting with no feed, fast sideways moves below the top of the sheet, cuts outside the sheet, wrong units, no tool or spindle, and cuts that do not go through. It also checks that every hole of the hole list lies inside its panel, keeps at least the smallest edge distance the drilling rules themselves use (the cup edge) from each edge, is not deeper than the panel is thick, and lists the sizes still blank. First machine run checklist is one printable page: the programs, what the dry run saw, what to check before the sheet goes on and in the first cuts, empty boxes and a sign-off. It says that no run has been made. Maker's review sheet lists every size and rule the corner cabinets, angled and curved walls and drilling used, where each comes from (common practice, a rule of the 32 mm system, geometry, typed by you) and an empty \"maker confirms\" box and \"corrects to\" space for a cabinet maker. Not built The labels have a Code 128 bar code and no QR code. The hole list is a generic format, not a boring machine's own file. The display bodies have no joinery detail: a panel is a box. A cabinet with a curved front is made to order and is not in the cut list, the programs or the hole list."},{"slug":"bim-cabinetcraft-bimcraft","scope":"addon:cabinetcraft","title":"CabinetCraft in BimCraft","summary":"What CabinetCraft does in BimCraft: the room from the model, the cabinets as furniture elements on the level, a schedule read back from the model, and the plan on the level.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["cabinetcraft","bimcraft","cabinets","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"the-room-comes-from-the-model","text":"The room comes from the model","level":2},{"id":"the-cabinets-become-elements","text":"The cabinets become elements","level":2},{"id":"schedule-and-check-tied-to-the-model","text":"Schedule and check, tied to the model","level":2},{"id":"the-plan-on-the-level","text":"The plan on the level","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do-here","text":"What it does not do here","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-room-comes-from-the-model\"\u003EThe room comes from the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind rooms\u003C/span\u003E lists the rooms the walls of the open model enclose. Select walls in BimCraft first and the rooms that touch them come first. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this room\u003C/span\u003E makes the room the job&#039;s room, with the doors and windows near its edge as the job&#039;s openings, and remembers which level it is on. Doors and windows further than 600 mm from a wall are left out and counted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-cabinets-become-elements\"\u003EThe cabinets become elements\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/span\u003E makes every cabinet, filler and end panel a piece of furniture (an IfcFurniture; in an IFC2X3 model an IfcFurnishingElement) and every countertop piece a building element proxy. They stand on the level the room came from, in the room&#039;s place. Each has a solid made of one prism per panel, coloured by the material pick, and a property set called CabinetCraft with its number, template, run, row, size, height above the floor, material and the job. It is one undo step; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace in the model\u003C/span\u003E replaces this job&#039;s elements and nothing else; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove from the model\u003C/span\u003E takes them out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"schedule-and-check-tied-to-the-model\"\u003ESchedule and check, tied to the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchedule from the model (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E reads the schedule back from the elements that are in the model, so it shows what is really there. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the model against the job\u003C/span\u003E compares the numbers in the model with the job and tells you what is missing or extra. The cut list is the job&#039;s own (the Export view); every element carries its cabinet number so the two can be matched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-plan-on-the-level\"\u003EThe plan on the level\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw the plan on the level\u003C/span\u003E draws the job&#039;s plan as drafting lines in the level&#039;s plan view, so it appears in the plan views and on the sheets BimCraft makes. Elevations and sections of the cabinets are BimCraft&#039;s own, cut from the elements; the job&#039;s own elevations and sections can be saved as a printable sheet set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do-here\"\u003EWhat it does not do here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no G-code in BimCraft (a CAM export is PartCraft&#039;s); you get the cut list, the nested DXF, the drilling lists and labels. The elements are made when you press the button: changing the job afterwards does not move them until you place again. Editing a cabinet in BimCraft does not change the job.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The room comes from the model On the Room tab, Find rooms lists the rooms the walls of the open model enclose. Select walls in BimCraft first and the rooms that touch them come first. Use this room makes the room the job's room, with the doors and windows near its edge as the job's openings, and remembers which level it is on. Doors and windows further than 600 mm from a wall are left out and counted. The cabinets become elements Drawings › Place in the model makes every cabinet, filler and end panel a piece of furniture (an IfcFurniture; in an IFC2X3 model an IfcFurnishingElement) and every countertop piece a building element proxy. They stand on the level the room came from, in the room's place. Each has a solid made of one prism per panel, coloured by the material pick, and a property set called CabinetCraft with its number, template, run, row, size, height above the floor, material and the job. It is one undo step; Replace in the model replaces this job's elements and nothing else; Remove from the model takes them out. Schedule and check, tied to the model Export › Schedule from the model (CSV) reads the schedule back from the elements that are in the model, so it shows what is really there. Check the model against the job compares the numbers in the model with the job and tells you what is missing or extra. The cut list is the job's own (the Export view); every element carries its cabinet number so the two can be matched. The plan on the level Draw the plan on the level draws the job's plan as drafting lines in the level's plan view, so it appears in the plan views and on the sheets BimCraft makes. Elevations and sections of the cabinets are BimCraft's own, cut from the elements; the job's own elevations and sections can be saved as a printable sheet set in Drawings. What it does not do here There is no G-code in BimCraft (a CAM export is PartCraft's); you get the cut list, the nested DXF, the drilling lists and labels. The elements are made when you press the button: changing the job afterwards does not move them until you place again. Editing a cabinet in BimCraft does not change the job."},{"slug":"cabinetcraft-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:cabinetcraft","title":"CabinetCraft: not built yet","summary":"What CabinetCraft does not do yet, so you can plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","roadmap","cabinetcraft","machine run","maker"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet-and-not-done-by-software","text":"Not built yet, and not done by software","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet-and-not-done-by-software\"\u003ENot built yet, and not done by software\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA machine run.\u003C/strong\u003E Nobody has cut a sheet from these programs or drilled from these hole lists on a machine. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDry run\u003C/span\u003E reads the files and finds what it can, and the first-run checklist is for the person who makes the first run. The run itself is yours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA cabinet maker&#039;s review.\u003C/strong\u003E The corner cabinets&#039; sizes, the closing panels, the mitres, how shelves rest at a corner and the curved-wall rules are common practice and geometry, not any maker&#039;s hardware. The maker&#039;s review sheet lists every one with a box for the maker to confirm or correct.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour shop&#039;s rates.\u003C/strong\u003E Every rate is a placeholder until you type or import your own. The estimate is a draft until every category in use is yours, and only then is it called a final quote.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMachine files for a boring machine.\u003C/strong\u003E The holes are a CSV and a generic XML list described in the export topic. They are not any machine&#039;s own format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFixing and lazy susan sizes.\u003C/strong\u003E The size of a dowel or cam fixing and of a lazy susan&#039;s shaft and pivot depends on the hardware you chose, so the holes are in the list with the size left blank until you type it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrilling sizes you type\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECurved-front cabinets as a cut list.\u003C/strong\u003E A cabinet with a curved front is made to order: it has a body in the drawings and viewport and a placeholder line in the estimate, but no cut list and no drilling.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECorner cabinets on a curve.\u003C/strong\u003E Corner cabinets need two straight walls. Next to a curved wall use the straight cabinets stepped along the curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe cabinets as part of the solid.\u003C/strong\u003E In the PartCraft viewport they are display bodies: they add nothing to the part&#039;s mass, STL or STEP.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EG-code outside PartCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft, CadCraft and ModelCraft have no CAM export. There you get the cut list, the nested sheets as DXF, the drilling lists and the labels; open the DXF in PartCraft&#039;s Manufacture for G-code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECabinets that follow the model.\u003C/strong\u003E In BimCraft, CadCraft and ModelCraft the cabinets are made from the job when you press the button; editing them there does not change the job, and changing the job does not move them until you place or draw again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDoors that swing out of a run&#039;s way.\u003C/strong\u003E A door in a wall is a clear span with a swing check, not a model of the door. On a curved wall it sits at a position along the chord.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQR labels.\u003C/strong\u003E The labels have a Code 128 bar code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther languages.\u003C/strong\u003E Spanish (Mexico) is a draft; the rest of the words are in English for now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet, and not done by software A machine run. Nobody has cut a sheet from these programs or drilled from these hole lists on a machine. Dry run reads the files and finds what it can, and the first-run checklist is for the person who makes the first run. The run itself is yours. A cabinet maker's review. The corner cabinets' sizes, the closing panels, the mitres, how shelves rest at a corner and the curved-wall rules are common practice and geometry, not any maker's hardware. The maker's review sheet lists every one with a box for the maker to confirm or correct. Your shop's rates. Every rate is a placeholder until you type or import your own. The estimate is a draft until every category in use is yours, and only then is it called a final quote. Machine files for a boring machine. The holes are a CSV and a generic XML list described in the export topic. They are not any machine's own format. Fixing and lazy susan sizes. The size of a dowel or cam fixing and of a lazy susan's shaft and pivot depends on the hardware you chose, so the holes are in the list with the size left blank until you type it under Export › Drilling sizes you type. Curved-front cabinets as a cut list. A cabinet with a curved front is made to order: it has a body in the drawings and viewport and a placeholder line in the estimate, but no cut list and no drilling. Corner cabinets on a curve. Corner cabinets need two straight walls. Next to a curved wall use the straight cabinets stepped along the curve. The cabinets as part of the solid. In the PartCraft viewport they are display bodies: they add nothing to the part's mass, STL or STEP. G-code outside PartCraft. BimCraft, CadCraft and ModelCraft have no CAM export. There you get the cut list, the nested sheets as DXF, the drilling lists and the labels; open the DXF in PartCraft's Manufacture for G-code. Cabinets that follow the model. In BimCraft, CadCraft and ModelCraft the cabinets are made from the job when you press the button; editing them there does not change the job, and changing the job does not move them until you place or draw again. Doors that swing out of a run's way. A door in a wall is a clear span with a swing check, not a model of the door. On a curved wall it sits at a position along the chord. QR labels. The labels have a Code 128 bar code. Other languages. Spanish (Mexico) is a draft; the rest of the words are in English for now."}]};
